5)00046-1","pdf":"","k":["Task (project management)","Set (abstract data type)","Computer science","Natural (archaeology)","Range (aeronautics)","Control (management)","Human–computer interaction","Cognitive psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00034-8","t":"Standing stomachs, clamoring chests and cooling livers: Metaphors in the psychological lexicon of Japanese","a":["Brian J. McVeigh"],"d":"1996-07-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00034-8","pdf":"","k":["Lexicon","Embodied cognition","Literal and figurative language","Metaphor","Psychology","Representation (politics)","Linguistics","Mental lexicon"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00039-9","t":"The Italian indicativo imperfetto compared to the Finnish conditional verb form — Evidence from child language","a":["Anneli Kauppinen"],"d":"1996-07-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00039-9","pdf":"","k":["Verb","Linguistics","Psychology","Philosophy"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(96)90027-2","t":"IPrA news service","a":[],"d":"1996-07-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(96)90027-2","pdf":"","k":["Service (business)","Advertising","Business","Marketing"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00023-2","t":"Some reflections on o sea: A discourse marker in Spanish","a":["Scott A. Schwenter"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"This paper examines the Spanish discourse marker o sea; which consists of the preposition o ‘or’ and sea, the third person singular, present subjunctive form of the verb ser ‘to be’. The uses of o sea are grouped into two broad classes: connective and epistemic. Connective uses guide hearers to the correct semantic interpretation between propositions. Epistemic uses mark speakers' degree of commitment to what they say, increasing the modal content of their utterances. In addition, there are also instances of o sea that appear to carry out both connective and epistemic functions. These latter uses challenge the typology of Fraser (1990), who distinguishes discourse markers from other pragmatic markers along similar lines. It is also argued, contra Fraser (1990), that the pragmatic meaning of discourse markers cannot be analyzed without reference to the content meaning of their lexical sources.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0378216695000232/pdf","pdf":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0378216695000232/pdf","k":["Linguistics","Typology","Meaning (existential)","Discourse marker","Verb","Interpretation (philosophy)","Modal","Philosophy"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00027-5","t":"Codeswitching in Hemnesberget — Myth or reality?","a":["Brit Mæhlum"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00027-5","pdf":"","k":["Norwegian","Repertoire","Focus (optics)","Meaning (existential)","Linguistics","Mythology","Sociology","Variety (cybernetics)"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00011-9","t":"The role of deictics in discourse coherence: French voici/voilà and Russian vot/von","a":["Lenore A. Grenoble","M. F. Riley"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"Both French and Russian utilize a binary system of deictic presentatives, most commonly described on the basis of ±proximal. However, in actual usage, one deictic is systematically favored over the other and can be seen as unmarked. The remaining member of the deictic pair is thus marked and less frequent. However, French and Russian differ in which member of the pair has been marked: in French the +proximal presentative is marked and in Russian the −proximal is marked. In both languages, the choice between the members of the pair is made on the basis of the proximity of the object presented relative to the deictic center. Moreover, these simple presentatives have taken on broader discourse functions which separate their usage from the qualities of the immediate complement and depend instead on the wider context of the discourse. Not only are these elements used to organize and mark the progression of a discourse, but they are used as active interactions between the speaker and the receiver, assuring that both are assisting in building the frame of reference of the discourse and aiding in local cohesion and global coherence of the discourse.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00011-9","pdf":"","k":["Deixis","Linguistics","Cohesion (chemistry)","Coherence (philosophical gambling strategy)","Object (grammar)","Context (archaeology)","Sociology","History"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00008-9","t":"The (nonvacuous) semantics of TE-linkage in Japanese","a":["Yoko Hasegawa"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"","oa":"green","url":"https://escholarship.org/content/qt0wr7b4wz/qt0wr7b4wz.pdf","pdf":"https://escholarship.org/content/qt0wr7b4wz/qt0wr7b4wz.pdf","k":["Linkage (software)","Semantics (computer science)","Linguistics","Psychology","Philosophy","Computer science","Programming language","Genetics"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00018-6","t":"Pragmatic determinants of syntactic subject in English","a":["Myunghee Kim"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00018-6","pdf":"","k":["Linguistics","Subject (documents)","Psychology","Philosophy","Computer science","Library science"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00055-0","t":"The pragmatics of emancipation: A critical review of the work of Michael Huspek","a":["Chik Collins"],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-2166(95)00055-0","pdf":"","k":["Pragmatics","Emancipation","Argument (complex analysis)","Epistemology","Criticism","Sociology","Applied linguistics","Theoretical linguistics"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(96)90077-6","t":"IPrA news service","a":[],"d":"1996-06-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(96)90077-6","pdf":"","k":["Service (business)","Business","Marketing"]},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-2166(95)00010-0","t":"Preferred argument struct